{
  "id": "5hhv7qi",
  "title": "Chapter 7: Electricity and Magnetism",
  "order": 56,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "a3r4kmj",
      "content": "Electricity and magnetism are closely related branches of physics. Electricity deals with the flow of electric charges, while magnetism deals with the magnetic properties of materials and magnetic fields produced by electric currents."
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "SI Unit of Current",
          "value": "Ampere (A)",
          "icon": "check"
        },
        {
          "label": "Ohm's Law Formula",
          "value": "V = IR",
          "icon": "book"
        },
        {
          "label": "Commercial Energy Unit",
          "value": "1 kWh = 3.6 MJ",
          "icon": "clock"
        },
        {
          "label": "SI Unit of Power",
          "value": "Watt (W)",
          "icon": "award"
        }
      ],
      "id": "bhx95hj"
    },
    {
      "id": "fqwpa06",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Electricity"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Electric current is defined as the rate of flow of electric charge (I = Q/t).",
        "Ohm's Law establishes that voltage (V) is directly proportional to current (I) across a conductor at a constant temperature.",
        "Resistance depends on the material, length, cross-sectional area, and temperature of the conductor.",
        "Electric circuits can be arranged in Series (same current) or Parallel (same voltage across branches).",
        "An electromagnet acts as a temporary magnet when electric current passes through a solenoid containing a soft iron core."
      ],
      "id": "3hsczoz"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "lwsxcur",
      "content": "Electricity is the flow of electric charge through a conductor. Electric current is the movement of electrons through a conductor such as copper wire."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "2dpkgp3",
      "content": "• Electric charge is a fundamental property of matter."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "wspd598",
      "content": "• Electric current is the flow of electric charge."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "4wiczb5",
      "content": "• The SI unit of electric current is Ampere (A)."
    },
    {
      "id": "jw01959",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. Electric Current"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "fm9u1hc",
      "content": "Electric current is defined as the rate of flow of electric charge."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "uz7uleg",
      "content": "Formula: I = Q / t"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "4zgjmte",
      "content": "Where:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "c5bkrzv",
      "content": "I = Electric current"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "hniecna",
      "content": "Q = Electric charge"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "k965sou",
      "content": "t = Time"
    },
    {
      "id": "md9o68x",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3. Voltage"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "io7cotb",
      "content": "Voltage is the potential difference between two points in an electric circuit."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "g8qf7c1",
      "content": "SI Unit of Voltage = Volt (V)"
    },
    {
      "id": "fpua0wj",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4. Resistance"
    },
    {
      "type": "pullQuote",
      "content": "Chapter 7: Electricity and Magnetism",
      "id": "ci6pv4j"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "dtui7s5",
      "content": "Resistance is the opposition offered by a conductor to the flow of electric current."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "o5mfvsi",
      "content": "SI Unit of Resistance = Ohm (Ω)"
    },
    {
      "id": "wdnyq2a",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5. Ohm’s Law"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ehofsao",
      "content": "Ohm’s Law states that the current flowing through a conductor is directly proportional to the voltage across it, provided temperature remains constant."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "hfis9fy",
      "content": "Formula: V = IR"
    },
    {
      "id": "jm4lhgw",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "6. Electric Power"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "s7uuy8r",
      "content": "Electric power is the rate at which electrical energy is consumed."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "tr5g1m8",
      "content": "Formula: P = VI"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "e46oj1c",
      "content": "SI Unit = Watt (W)"
    },
    {
      "id": "u0evy4v",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "7. Types of Electric Circuits"
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
      "headers": [
        "Feature",
        "Series Circuit",
        "Parallel Circuit"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Current Flow",
          "Same current flows through all components",
          "Current divides among parallel branches"
        ],
        [
          "Voltage",
